WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.660 --> 00:00:07.010
- Good afternoon, everyone.

00:00:07.940 --> 00:00:11.030
Marta and I are heartened
to see you on our screens.

00:00:11.030 --> 00:00:13.390
And we're joined today by Secretary

00:00:13.390 --> 00:00:17.203
and Vice President of University
Life, Kimberly Goff-Crews,

00:00:18.052 --> 00:00:22.620
Yuliia Zhukovets, a junior
in Franklin College,

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:25.173
and University Chaplain, Sharon Kugler.

00:00:26.430 --> 00:00:28.550
To everyone online, thank
you for taking the time

00:00:28.550 --> 00:00:31.660
to participate in this vigil for peace.

00:00:31.660 --> 00:00:33.710
All of us recognize

00:00:33.710 --> 00:00:36.070
that the world is in a
somber, perilous place.

00:00:36.070 --> 00:00:39.770
And over the past few days,
I've watched with horror,

00:00:39.770 --> 00:00:44.293
as I know you have, Vladimir
Putin's invasion of Ukraine.

00:00:45.630 --> 00:00:49.910
Putin's aggression against a
sovereign independent nation

00:00:49.910 --> 00:00:53.563
raises the specter of prolonged
conflict throughout Europe.

00:00:55.190 --> 00:00:57.630
This is the largest
ground attack in Europe

00:00:57.630 --> 00:00:58.753
since World War II.

00:01:00.110 --> 00:01:03.070
We know from history that
the risks of miscalculation

00:01:03.070 --> 00:01:04.483
are dangerously high.

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:07.480
And at Yale, we are compelled

00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:10.000
by our common sense of humanity

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:13.073
to reject this violence
in the strongest terms.

00:01:14.170 --> 00:01:16.120
We stand for peace.

00:01:16.120 --> 00:01:18.503
We grieve for the lives lost.

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:22.820
We also support the students and scholars

00:01:22.820 --> 00:01:25.150
of the Yale Ukrainian community,

00:01:25.150 --> 00:01:26.660
and those Yale students and scholars

00:01:26.660 --> 00:01:30.150
from elsewhere in the
region, including Russia,

00:01:30.150 --> 00:01:33.113
who I am confident do not
support this invasion.

00:01:34.910 --> 00:01:36.540
I know many of our students and colleagues

00:01:36.540 --> 00:01:38.680
are shocked and distressed.

00:01:38.680 --> 00:01:40.090
They fear for the future

00:01:40.090 --> 00:01:42.503
and for the safety of
loved ones and friends.

00:01:43.810 --> 00:01:46.160
Over the past week, Yale staff and faculty

00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:48.853
have been reaching out
to them to offer support.

00:01:50.540 --> 00:01:51.373
For example,

00:01:51.373 --> 00:01:53.240
the Office of International
Students and Scholars

00:01:53.240 --> 00:01:56.180
is working with each affected student.

00:01:56.180 --> 00:01:58.970
They're helping them with
post-graduation plans,

00:01:58.970 --> 00:02:02.580
employment authorizations,
and travel needs.

00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:05.350
We're monitoring developments
regarding immigration options

00:02:05.350 --> 00:02:08.923
and sharing with students and
scholars relevant updates.

00:02:10.210 --> 00:02:13.190
For undergraduates from
Ukraine on financial aid,

00:02:13.190 --> 00:02:16.839
we will reevaluate the financial
aid awards they receive

00:02:16.839 --> 00:02:21.097
if there are changes in
your economic situation.

00:02:22.690 --> 00:02:25.860
We're advocating for policies
that protect the legal status

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:28.720
of Ukrainian students
and scholars in the US

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:30.593
during and beyond their studies here.

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:35.270
We're supporting consultations
with immigration attorneys

00:02:35.270 --> 00:02:38.320
for Ukrainian students and
scholars who need assistance

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:41.793
in navigating their future
status in the United States.

00:02:43.330 --> 00:02:45.770
In the days ahead, we'll continue to work

00:02:45.770 --> 00:02:48.720
with individual students
in any way we can.

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:51.420
In addition, faculty members on our campus

00:02:51.420 --> 00:02:54.583
are helping policymakers
think through how to respond.

00:02:56.050 --> 00:02:58.290
The coming weeks, the Jackson Institute

00:02:58.290 --> 00:03:00.973
will be hosting an
expert panel discussion.

00:03:02.110 --> 00:03:03.570
We will hear from faculty members

00:03:03.570 --> 00:03:05.590
who have long studied
the history of the region

00:03:05.590 --> 00:03:07.940
and can share insights
into the current conflict,

00:03:07.940 --> 00:03:12.940
and anticipate what may come
next and how we can respond.

00:03:14.450 --> 00:03:15.930
I very much hope the scholars

00:03:15.930 --> 00:03:17.570
and creative thinkers of our community

00:03:17.570 --> 00:03:20.353
can help the world chart
a path toward peace.

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:25.240
Right now, the spirit of
division around the globe

00:03:25.240 --> 00:03:27.460
seems stronger than ever.

00:03:27.460 --> 00:03:31.083
We must respond by embracing
the ways we are connected.

00:03:32.360 --> 00:03:34.160
Not all of us work or study in fields

00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:36.310
that can contribute solutions directly

00:03:36.310 --> 00:03:39.293
to global conflicts of this kind,

00:03:40.340 --> 00:03:42.530
but all of us play vital roles

00:03:42.530 --> 00:03:46.813
in creating an environment of
peace and belonging at Yale,

00:03:47.740 --> 00:03:50.593
and within all communities
of which we are a part.

00:03:52.350 --> 00:03:54.290
So let us treat one another and ourselves

00:03:54.290 --> 00:03:56.510
with kindness and understanding.

00:03:56.510 --> 00:03:57.980
Let us approach our lives

00:03:57.980 --> 00:04:00.740
with gratitude and with mindfulness.

00:04:00.740 --> 00:04:04.130
During this time of
crisis, it gives me hope

00:04:04.130 --> 00:04:05.960
when I see our community's commitment

00:04:05.960 --> 00:04:09.003
to one another and to peace.

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:13.413
Please keep in mind, in your minds,

00:04:15.500 --> 00:04:20.183
the families and friends who
are in the Ukraine right now,

00:04:22.360 --> 00:04:25.730
withstanding, and surviving,

00:04:25.730 --> 00:04:30.043
and combating this unjustified barrage.

00:04:31.610 --> 00:04:33.290
Thank you for joining us.

00:04:33.290 --> 00:04:35.686
I'll now turn things over to Secretary

00:04:35.686 --> 00:04:39.793
and Vice President for University
Life, Kimberly Goff-Crews.

00:04:41.983 --> 00:04:42.816
- Thank you, President Salovey.

00:04:44.390 --> 00:04:46.660
I join the president in welcoming everyone

00:04:46.660 --> 00:04:48.333
to this vigil for peace.

00:04:49.210 --> 00:04:52.770
We are here to support
our Ukraine faculty,

00:04:52.770 --> 00:04:54.690
students, staff, and alumni,

00:04:54.690 --> 00:04:57.180
as well as those with ties to Ukraine,

00:04:57.180 --> 00:05:00.180
and to find strength in one another.

00:05:00.180 --> 00:05:03.430
Some of you may have been at
the rally on campus today,

00:05:03.430 --> 00:05:06.780
and I know that there are other
events going on this week,

00:05:06.780 --> 00:05:08.823
including one today with the symphony.

00:05:09.670 --> 00:05:12.100
Tonight, we create a virtual space

00:05:12.100 --> 00:05:13.790
to join people around the world

00:05:13.790 --> 00:05:17.853
in praying for an end to war
in Ukraine and for peace.

00:05:19.140 --> 00:05:22.850
We know that events that
seem rather distanced

00:05:22.850 --> 00:05:24.350
have global effects.

00:05:24.350 --> 00:05:25.730
They can touch people around us

00:05:25.730 --> 00:05:28.500
and the people who are important to them.

00:05:28.500 --> 00:05:31.980
Of course, we do not need to
have personal ties to Ukraine

00:05:31.980 --> 00:05:36.140
to feel shock, fear, and
anger, and to wish for peace.

00:05:36.140 --> 00:05:37.840
Some of you here this evening

00:05:37.840 --> 00:05:40.800
may have experienced
violence or war yourself.

00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:43.263
We are grateful for your
presence and solidarity.

00:05:44.650 --> 00:05:47.470
We're profoundly affected
by this week's events

00:05:47.470 --> 00:05:49.840
because we care for humanity

00:05:49.840 --> 00:05:52.753
in Ukraine, in the world, and at Yale.

00:05:53.760 --> 00:05:56.780
So I encourage all of us to
look out for one another,

00:05:56.780 --> 00:05:59.680
especially people in your
social communities, classes,

00:05:59.680 --> 00:06:02.813
and work settings as we
process what is happening.

00:06:04.090 --> 00:06:07.600
If you need it, I encourage
you to seek support,

00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:10.300
whether from your school
or residential college,

00:06:10.300 --> 00:06:13.210
mental health and counseling,
the chaplain's office,

00:06:13.210 --> 00:06:15.190
our employee assistance programs,

00:06:15.190 --> 00:06:17.153
or of course, from friends and family.

00:06:18.480 --> 00:06:19.970
As these events unfold,

00:06:19.970 --> 00:06:21.810
we will support members of our community

00:06:21.810 --> 00:06:24.240
from Ukraine, Russia, and the region

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:27.240
in the ways that President
Salovey described.

00:06:27.240 --> 00:06:28.330
We've got you.

00:06:28.330 --> 00:06:29.230
We really got you.

00:06:30.146 --> 00:06:34.000
And we will continue Yale's
important work to serve humanity

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:37.753
and to make for a safer,
healthier, more equitable world.

00:06:39.300 --> 00:06:41.020
Before I turn to our next speaker,

00:06:41.020 --> 00:06:41.853
I would like to share

00:06:41.853 --> 00:06:43.900
some of Martin Luther King Jr.'s remarks

00:06:43.900 --> 00:06:47.463
when he received the Nobel Prize in 1964.

00:06:48.390 --> 00:06:51.320
He was speaking about
the nuclear arms race.

00:06:51.320 --> 00:06:53.343
His words have resonance tonight.

00:06:54.700 --> 00:06:58.120
He said, "So we must fix our vision

00:06:58.120 --> 00:07:01.170
not merely on the
negative expulsion of war,

00:07:01.170 --> 00:07:04.470
but upon the positive
affirmation of peace.

00:07:04.470 --> 00:07:07.780
We must see that peace
represents a sweeter music,

00:07:07.780 --> 00:07:11.793
a cosmic melody that is far
superior to the discords of war.

00:07:12.840 --> 00:07:15.330
Somehow we must transform the dynamics

00:07:15.330 --> 00:07:16.950
of the world's power struggle

00:07:16.950 --> 00:07:19.710
from a negative to a positive contest

00:07:19.710 --> 00:07:22.290
to harness man's creative genius

00:07:22.290 --> 00:07:25.160
for the purpose of making
peace and prosperity

00:07:25.160 --> 00:07:27.763
a reality for all of the
nations of the world.

00:07:28.970 --> 00:07:30.570
If we have the will and determination

00:07:30.570 --> 00:07:34.457
to mount such peace to such
a peace offensive," he said,

00:07:34.457 --> 00:07:38.770
"We will unlock tightly
sealed doors of hope

00:07:38.770 --> 00:07:41.630
and transform our imminent cosmic elegy

00:07:41.630 --> 00:07:44.127
into a psalm of creative fulfillment."

00:07:46.080 --> 00:07:48.630
Again, thank you for joining us.

00:07:48.630 --> 00:07:51.357
I will now turn to Yuliia Zhukovets,

00:07:51.357 --> 00:07:54.203
a junior in Yale College
and a citizen of Ukraine.

00:07:56.417 --> 00:07:58.120
- Hi, everyone.

00:07:58.120 --> 00:08:00.430
Thank you, Peter, and thank you, Kimberly

00:08:00.430 --> 00:08:02.040
for everything you said.

00:08:02.040 --> 00:08:04.840
My name is Yuliia and
I'm a junior in Franklin,

00:08:04.840 --> 00:08:08.203
and I was born and
raised in Kyiv, Ukraine.

00:08:09.266 --> 00:08:13.270
As President Salovey and
Kimberly have already mentioned,

00:08:13.270 --> 00:08:16.750
an unthinkable is happening right now

00:08:16.750 --> 00:08:19.470
in my home country in Ukraine.

00:08:19.470 --> 00:08:23.250
On the 24th of February at 5:00 am,

00:08:23.250 --> 00:08:26.060
Putin invaded Ukraine,
invaded my home country,

00:08:26.060 --> 00:08:28.520
and forever changed my life

00:08:28.520 --> 00:08:31.273
and lives of millions of other Ukrainians.

00:08:32.290 --> 00:08:36.270
I was lucky enough to not be
there when everything started

00:08:36.270 --> 00:08:37.500
and not to be there right now,

00:08:37.500 --> 00:08:40.570
and I understand and
acknowledge how lucky I am,

00:08:40.570 --> 00:08:44.420
but millions of people
who are still in Ukraine

00:08:44.420 --> 00:08:45.733
are not that lucky.

00:08:46.930 --> 00:08:50.350
Everyone who is still in the major cities

00:08:50.350 --> 00:08:54.580
and in the areas of
active military actions

00:08:54.580 --> 00:08:58.940
have to spend days and nights
hiding in the bomb shelters,

00:08:58.940 --> 00:09:00.900
trying to protect themselves

00:09:00.900 --> 00:09:04.230
against the Russian missile attacks.

00:09:04.230 --> 00:09:09.210
However, the Ukrainian army
has been extremely successful

00:09:09.210 --> 00:09:12.070
with fighting the Russian forces.

00:09:12.070 --> 00:09:15.570
We won most of the ground battles,

00:09:15.570 --> 00:09:19.580
and we continue to stop
the Russian invasion

00:09:19.580 --> 00:09:21.000
and continue to stop them

00:09:21.000 --> 00:09:23.263
against progressing
further into the country.

00:09:24.920 --> 00:09:27.610
It is really tough being away from home

00:09:27.610 --> 00:09:31.980
and not being able to
be there for my family,

00:09:31.980 --> 00:09:36.540
but I hope as most of you know,

00:09:36.540 --> 00:09:41.140
there was a 2:00 pm rally
for Ukraine across campus,

00:09:41.140 --> 00:09:45.030
and by this, I'm trying
to support my country,

00:09:45.030 --> 00:09:47.190
support my family, and do everything I can

00:09:47.190 --> 00:09:49.350
being thousands of miles away.

00:09:49.350 --> 00:09:53.190
I have received incredible
support from the administration,

00:09:53.190 --> 00:09:57.750
from my fellow students, from
other Ukrainians at Yale,

00:09:57.750 --> 00:10:00.926
and Yale math school,
and Yale Divinity School,

00:10:00.926 --> 00:10:03.000
and from Yale New Haven Hospital.

00:10:03.000 --> 00:10:06.930
The support that Yale has
given me has been incredible,

00:10:06.930 --> 00:10:10.590
and I cannot imagine a
better place to be right now.

00:10:10.590 --> 00:10:14.625
I urge all of you to take a minute today

00:10:14.625 --> 00:10:19.300
to think how lucky and privileged you are

00:10:19.300 --> 00:10:21.830
to be away from the war right now,

00:10:21.830 --> 00:10:24.723
and how lucky you are to be in the US,

00:10:25.730 --> 00:10:27.686
or wherever you are right
now where you're attending,

00:10:27.686 --> 00:10:32.230
and not to be in the active military zone

00:10:32.230 --> 00:10:35.550
where the war against humanity,

00:10:35.550 --> 00:10:38.840
the war against one of the
oldest nations in Europe,

00:10:38.840 --> 00:10:43.633
the genocide of Ukrainian
people is happening right now.

00:10:44.540 --> 00:10:46.060
Thank you all for coming here.

00:10:46.060 --> 00:10:47.500
This means a lot to me.

00:10:47.500 --> 00:10:49.870
This means a lot to my family.

00:10:49.870 --> 00:10:51.000
Thank you for tuning in.

00:10:51.000 --> 00:10:55.110
And I'm gonna pass it to Sharon Kugler,

00:10:55.110 --> 00:10:56.330
the University Chaplain.

00:10:56.330 --> 00:10:57.163
Thank you.

00:10:59.010 --> 00:11:00.690
- Thank you so much, Yuliia.

00:11:00.690 --> 00:11:03.750
You've shown such grace
and strength this last week

00:11:03.750 --> 00:11:05.483
and our prayers are with you.

00:11:06.510 --> 00:11:09.140
My beloved Yale family.

00:11:09.140 --> 00:11:12.600
this is a vigil calling for peace,

00:11:12.600 --> 00:11:16.430
and my words are intended
to both amplify that call

00:11:16.430 --> 00:11:18.123
and turn it into action.

00:11:19.170 --> 00:11:22.920
Our world, this beautiful
yet imperfect world

00:11:22.920 --> 00:11:27.233
that we all share, breaks a
little bit more every day.

00:11:28.130 --> 00:11:32.340
We have been living with a
deep burden of global unrest,

00:11:32.340 --> 00:11:37.170
uncertainty, grief, and fear
that is starting to feel

00:11:37.170 --> 00:11:40.113
brutally defiant in its staying power.

00:11:41.190 --> 00:11:44.883
Our spirits are suffering
under the stress of it all.

00:11:45.740 --> 00:11:49.400
And now, here we are,
gathered in this imperfect way

00:11:49.400 --> 00:11:53.810
across these many screens,
longing for connection,

00:11:53.810 --> 00:11:58.810
for reasons to hope, longing
for multiple kinds of peace.

00:11:59.900 --> 00:12:04.160
What can we do thousands of
miles away from a new war,

00:12:04.160 --> 00:12:06.853
a new invasion, to bring peace?

00:12:07.820 --> 00:12:11.660
This is a vigil calling for peace.

00:12:11.660 --> 00:12:15.900
Peace in Ukraine where
people are fleeing, hiding,

00:12:15.900 --> 00:12:18.640
or preparing to lose their very lives

00:12:18.640 --> 00:12:21.683
in defense of their
home, their way of life.

00:12:22.740 --> 00:12:25.710
This is a vigil calling for peace.

00:12:25.710 --> 00:12:30.710
Peace in all places where
aggression, poverty, ignorance,

00:12:31.670 --> 00:12:36.023
and violence oppresses and
destroys our human family.

00:12:38.130 --> 00:12:41.220
This is a vigil calling for peace.

00:12:41.220 --> 00:12:43.660
Peace in our aching hearts,

00:12:43.660 --> 00:12:46.860
so that we can find the
strength to keep going

00:12:46.860 --> 00:12:50.853
and to be part of a kind of
healing that is restorative.

00:12:52.410 --> 00:12:55.500
This is a vigil calling for peace.

00:12:55.500 --> 00:12:58.540
Peace in our minds so that we can use them

00:12:58.540 --> 00:13:01.250
to create a more just world

00:13:01.250 --> 00:13:04.440
where power of means or might is used

00:13:04.440 --> 00:13:09.103
to elevate the human condition
and to cherish our planet.

00:13:10.320 --> 00:13:13.660
This is a vigil calling for peace.

00:13:13.660 --> 00:13:16.510
Peace in our relationships with others,

00:13:16.510 --> 00:13:19.230
where we can see and be seen

00:13:19.230 --> 00:13:21.710
for the uniquely beautiful mysteries

00:13:21.710 --> 00:13:24.453
contained in each of us.

00:13:25.920 --> 00:13:29.540
This is a vigil calling for peace.

00:13:29.540 --> 00:13:34.130
Peace in the light, peace in the dark,

00:13:34.130 --> 00:13:38.780
peace in the big, peace in the small,

00:13:38.780 --> 00:13:42.923
peace in the weak, peace in the strong.

00:13:44.430 --> 00:13:47.293
This is a vigil calling for peace.

00:13:49.170 --> 00:13:52.780
One of my favorite prayers is also a hymn.

00:13:52.780 --> 00:13:55.080
It's the Prayer of St. Francis.

00:13:55.080 --> 00:13:58.570
In its entirety, it's a
distinctly Christian prayer.

00:13:58.570 --> 00:14:02.550
However, the first part feels
accessible to all people,

00:14:02.550 --> 00:14:07.003
and it is in this moment, a
much needed balm for the soul.

00:14:08.040 --> 00:14:12.500
The prayer asks for us to be
instruments of God's peace.

00:14:12.500 --> 00:14:17.373
The prayer reveals that
making peace is in our hands.

00:14:18.810 --> 00:14:22.500
Lord, make me an instrument of your peace.

00:14:22.500 --> 00:14:26.120
Where there is hatred, let me so love.

00:14:26.120 --> 00:14:29.270
Where there is injury, pardon.

00:14:29.270 --> 00:14:31.403
Where there is doubt, faith.

00:14:32.410 --> 00:14:35.033
Where there is despair, hope.

00:14:35.970 --> 00:14:38.583
Where there is darkness, light.

00:14:39.580 --> 00:14:42.693
Where there is sadness, joy.

00:14:44.560 --> 00:14:47.940
This is a vigil calling for peace.

00:14:47.940 --> 00:14:50.370
May you be shalom.

00:14:50.370 --> 00:14:52.670
May you be salaam.

00:14:52.670 --> 00:14:54.333
May you be shanti.

00:14:55.790 --> 00:14:57.593
May you bring peace.

00:14:59.930 --> 00:15:03.640
I now invite you to join me in
observing a moment of silence

00:15:03.640 --> 00:15:08.260
honoring those across the
world, particularly in Ukraine,

00:15:08.260 --> 00:15:11.400
who have been lost,
those who are grieving,

00:15:11.400 --> 00:15:14.723
and those living in fear for
what might be coming next.

00:15:15.740 --> 00:15:18.620
Focus simply on being still.

00:15:18.620 --> 00:15:21.020
Let your mind turn to what it might mean

00:15:21.020 --> 00:15:23.613
to be an instrument of peace.

00:16:09.700 --> 00:16:10.623
Thank you.

00:16:11.600 --> 00:16:14.690
This concludes the formal
part of this vigil.

00:16:14.690 --> 00:16:17.910
I invite you to linger a
bit in this Zoom space.

00:16:17.910 --> 00:16:20.180
We have arranged for a
couple of pieces of music

00:16:20.180 --> 00:16:22.650
to be played to soothe your soul.

00:16:22.650 --> 00:16:24.590
Let the music bless you,

00:16:24.590 --> 00:16:27.100
and then please feel free
to leave when you are ready,

00:16:27.100 --> 00:16:28.680
and perhaps seek out a friend

00:16:28.680 --> 00:16:31.370
to connect with over dinner this evening,

00:16:31.370 --> 00:16:34.090
call a family member just to check in,

00:16:34.090 --> 00:16:36.990
or introduce yourself to someone new,

00:16:36.990 --> 00:16:40.000
and be an instrument of peace.

00:16:40.000 --> 00:16:42.403
Thank you so much for joining us today.

00:16:43.260 --> 00:16:47.663
Peace be with you and God
bless the people of Ukraine.

